From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Mon, 09 May 94 01:30:37 GMT
Subject: [#94-5-99] SCI: Chaos, Complexity and Reductionism

Reilly Jones writes:
>Science that relys on reductionism alone is half-intelligible.  The other half
>of science that completes the picture and makes it intelligible is holism.  Of
>course, holism without reductionism is equally half-intelligible.

Yes, some models would only give half the picture.  The holism, though,
may be built up scientifically or mystically.

>Without fully utilizing both methods, science is easily led into religious
>propositions such as theological creation ex nihilo theories like Big Bang
>cosmologies (one-ended tunnels with quantum fuzz... Pfeh!) and intellectually
>bankrupt solipsism like Many-Worlds interpretation of quantum mechanics.  When
>scientists only aspire to describe and not to explain (or vice versa), they can
>be dismissed out of hand as weakling, only half-intelligible.

An example of a model which can only be half-intelligible is that of the
Newtonian mechanistic monotrack universe.  Observed phenomena which are
sometimes described as "wavefunction collapse", "quantum randomness" and
"quantum non-locality" may be explained mystically, e.g. "the observer's
consciousness collapses the wavefunction" and "the universe is holistic".
The scientific materialist alternative would be something more on the
lines of:

The mind of each individual is comprised of many duplicate or near-
duplicate brains, each occupying its own world or universe.  Thus, at any
given point in time, each individual's mind occupies her own spread of a
myriad of parallel worlds, which we may term a multiverse.  Although there
is an overlap between the multiverses of several individuals, they will
almost certainly not totally concur.  The omniverse includes all existing
worlds.  (And this may be considered to include only the worlds of
everyone's multiverse and all those worlds which are necessary to account
for observed phenomena, or it may include every conceivable world that
could possibly exist.  I prefer the latter.)    

But postulating all these objective material worlds is the very opposite of
solipsism.  I'm not the only entity that exists -- all possible entities
exist, and they're just as real as I am.

And now that we have all these worlds, this allows us to have the
"universe" (better described as a consensual reality which emerges from
a compilation of everyone's multiverse) "spontaneously" coming into
existence from a quantum vacuum.  Worlds which are very similar on the
local macroscopic scale will share plenty of reference points in space and
time, so each point in space and time in one world is in parallel with the
corresponding point in each other world.  Although we don't know the exact
time of inflation and the Big Bang (we each occupy a multiverse where it
occurred at various times), it must have happened at a particular time in
each world or universe.  A multiverse would have worlds erupting into
existence at each point in time, but let's assume we could pin down the
exact time.

The omniverse still has worlds spontaneously arising at every instant in
time.  If we were able to say that inflation occurred
265,078,179,153,965,803 seconds ago, our near-duplicates would have found
it to be two seconds before, or ten seconds before, etc.  Speaking in terms
of an individual universe or even a multiverse whose time of creation was
accurately known, time begins at a particular time, therefore there is no
need to explain what happened "before" that time as there was no such time.
But speaking in terms of the omniverse, ten seconds before inflation and
the Big Bang the omniverse was a ferment of quantum activity, a seething
broth of virtual particles and erupting universes.

That which can exist, does exist, so each possible world comes into
existence "spontaneously" at the allotted time.  Since each particular
time was the time that the given universe came into existence, it didn't
really have much choice.  And maybe there is room for ageless infinite
universes in this model.  If we can't rule out infinite universes, then
the omniverse is of infinite age, and throughout this period, universes
were spontaneously erupting into existence.  But each individual only
observes those worlds which are a part of her multiverse.  The others,
like the rock removed by a sculptor, are out of sight and out of mind. 

